tel 1.0

base cataclysm
import soundness

info   CSS as typed values: stylesheets, selectors and properties checked against their real grammars
detail
    _Cataclysm_ represents CSS — stylesheets, their rules and selectors, and the values of
    their properties — as typed values, checked against the genuine grammar of each
    property rather than left for a browser to ignore. A browser never rejects CSS: a
    misspelled property or a malformed value fails by doing nothing. Here, the same mistake
    is a `Css.Error` when text is read at runtime, and a compile error when it is written
    in a `css"…"` literal or a `Css.Style(…)` constructor, both checked against the
    value-definition syntax of the MDN data for over six hundred properties.

    The central type is `Css`: a stylesheet as a list of `Css.Node`s — rules, declarations
    and at-rules — read from text with `read[Css]`, written with the `css"…"` interpolator,
    joined with `+`, and rendered with `show` under a `Css.Formatting` chosen by name from
    the `formatting` package. A rule's selector is a `SelectorList` of `Selector`s, each a
    `Compound` of `Simple` parts joined by `Combinator`s, covering the whole of Selectors
    Level 4. Property values are typed: lengths, angles, times and grid flex are quantities
    in CSS's own units (`4.0*Px`, `90.0*Deg`, `200.0*Ms`, `2.0*Fr`), a colour renders as a
    hex triplet, and the `Css.Convertible` typeclass says which native type is acceptable
    for which value-definition type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import formatting.indentedCssFormatting

    val red = Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
    val width = 4.0*Px

    val sheet: Css = css"a { color: $red; width: $width }"
    val inline: Css.Style = Css.Style(color = red, borderWidth = 2.0*Px)

    sheet.show                                  // a {\n  color: #ff0000;\n  width: 4px;\n}\n
    t"a { color: red }".read[Css] + sheet       // one stylesheet of both rules
    inline.text                                 // color: #ff0000; border-width: 2px
    ```

    Reading accumulates every fault — unknown properties, bad values, unterminated
    comments — into one `Css.Errors`, rather than stopping at the first, so a stylesheet is
    corrected in one pass. Errors need a `Diagnostics` in scope, such as
    `errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics`.

    The library is split into three source sets:

    - `cataclysm.core` — `Css` and its nodes, errors, keywords and `Css.Convertible`; the
      selector model (`SelectorList`, `Selector`, `Compound`, `Simple`, …); the property
      dataset (`PropertyDef`, `SyntaxMatcher`, `Css.Syntax`, `ValueToken`, `Outcome`); the
      units (`Px`, `Rem`, `Vw`, `Pct`, `Deg`, `Fr`, …); the `css"…"` interpolator; the
      `classes` and `ids` extensions; and the `formatting` and `construables` packages.
      Compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `cataclysm.fonts` — the `Web` medium and its typeface provisions (`Web.linked`,
      `Web.imported`, `Web.local`, the generic families), `FontFace`, `Css.fontFace` and
      `Css.fontFaces`, and the `style` extension which turns a phoenicia `Face` or `Font`
      into the `Css.Style` that selects it.
    - `cataclysm.html` — the honeycomb bindings: `inlineStyle` lets a `Css.Style` be a
      `style` attribute, and `Styles` with `cssBindings.checkedBinding` checks every class
      and id name in a template against a stylesheet on the classpath as the code compiles.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The `formatting`, `construables`
    and `cssBindings` packages merge with the umbrella's own, and their givens are imported
    by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens: a program writes
    `import formatting.indentedCssFormatting` (or `compactCssFormatting`) before rendering a
    stylesheet, and `import cssBindings.checkedBinding` to turn on checked class names.

refer cataclysm.Css
refer cataclysm.css
refer cataclysm.SelectorList
refer cataclysm.PropertyDef
refer cataclysm.Web
refer cataclysm.Styles
refer cataclysm.formatting

entry .Css   a stylesheet: a list of rules, declarations and at-rules, read, written, joined and rendered
  detail
      A `Css` is a stylesheet as a value: its `#rules` are `Css.Node`s, each a rule with a
      parsed `SelectorList` and a body, a declaration of a property and its value, or an
      at-rule with its name, prelude and optional body. It is made by reading text
      (`.aggregable`), by the `css"…"` interpolator, by `Css.fontFace` from a font, or
      directly from nodes; two stylesheets join with `+` (`.addable`) into one of both rule
      lists.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import formatting.compactCssFormatting

      val css: Css = t"a { color: red } @media screen { a { width: 4px } }".read[Css]
      css.rules.size              // 2
      css.show                    // t"a{color:red;}@media screen{a{width:4px;}}"
      css.classes                 // the class names its selectors mention
      ```

      Reading validates every declaration against the property's grammar and accumulates
      every problem into a `Css.Errors`, raised once at the end. Rendering goes through the
      `Css.Formatting` in scope — `formatting.indentedCssFormatting` puts each rule and
      declaration on its own indented line, `formatting.compactCssFormatting` strips every
      inessential character — as `show` (`.showable`), as a lazy `Stream[Text]`
      (`.streamable`), or as an HTTP `text/css` body (`.media`). In every style, a `;`
      follows each declaration, including the last.

      The companion also holds the CSS-wide keywords (`.inherit`, `.initial`, `.unset`,
      `.revert`, `.revertLayer`) and the colour keywords (`.transparent`, `.currentColor`)
      as typed values for substitution, the `.Style` constructor for inline styles, the
      `.Convertible` typeclass, the `.Syntax` grammar type and the `.Error`s.
  refer cataclysm.css
  refer cataclysm.SelectorList
  refer cataclysm.PropertyDef
  refer cataclysm.formatting
  entry .aggregable   reads a stylesheet from text, accumulating every `Css.Error` into one `Css.Errors`
      The given which lets text be `read[Css]`. The parse validates every declaration —
      the property must be known, and its value must satisfy the property's grammar — and
      runs under a `track`, so each `Css.Error` is accrued rather than stopping the parse,
      and a single `Css.Errors` holding all of them is raised at the end. A fully valid
      stylesheet gives the `Css` with nothing raised. It needs a `Tactic[Css.Errors]` and a
      `Diagnostics` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      t"a { color: red }".read[Css]
      capture[Css.Errors](t"a { colour: red; width: wide }".read[Css]).errors.map(_.reason)
      // List(Css.Error.Reason.UnknownProperty(t"colour"), Css.Error.Reason.BadValue(t"width", t"wide"))
      ```

      Custom properties (`--name`) are accepted without a definition. A value whose grammar
      uses a type the matcher does not implement is reported as `UnsupportedValue` rather
      than passed silently.
  entry .Formatting   how a stylesheet renders: with newlines and indentation, or compactly
    detail
        A `Css.Formatting` says whether rendering puts each rule and declaration on its own
        indented line (`#newlines`) and whether it adds the cosmetic spaces after `:` and
        before `{` (`#spaces`). The `Showable`, `Streamable` and `Media` instances of `Css`
        each take one as a contextual value, so one must be in scope to render: the two
        standard ones are `formatting.indentedCssFormatting` (both on) and
        `formatting.compactCssFormatting` (both off), imported by name, and
        `Css.Formatting(newlines, spaces)` makes any other.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        val css = t"a { color: red }".read[Css]

        locally:
          import formatting.indentedCssFormatting
          css.show                 // t"a {\n  color: red;\n}\n"

        locally:
          given Css.Formatting = Css.Formatting(newlines = true, spaces = false)
          css.show                 // t"a{\n  color:red;\n}\n"
        ```
    refer cataclysm.formatting
    entry .apply   a formatting with the given newline and spacing choices
    entry #newlines   whether each rule and declaration goes on its own indented line
    entry #spaces   whether a space follows each `:` and precedes each `{`
  entry .Node   one node of a stylesheet: a rule, a declaration or an at-rule
      The three kinds of node a stylesheet holds, as an enum. `Rule(selector, body)` is a
      rule with a parsed `SelectorList` and a body of further nodes, so nested rules are
      represented directly. `Declaration(property, value)` is one property and its value,
      both as text, with comments stripped and whitespace normalized. `At(name, prelude,
      body)` is an at-rule by its name (without the `@`), its prelude text, and its body,
      which is `Unset` for a statement at-rule such as `@import url("x.css");` and a list of
      nodes for a block at-rule such as `@media screen { … }`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val sheet = Css(List(
        Css.Node.Rule(SelectorList(List(Selector(Unset, Compound(List(Simple.Type(Unset, t"a"))), Nil))),
          List(Css.Node.Declaration(t"color", t"red"))),
        Css.Node.At(t"import", t"url(\"x.css\")", Unset)))
      ```

      Nodes built directly are not validated; `read[Css]` and `css"…"` are the checked
      routes.
  entry .streamable   renders a stylesheet lazily as a `Stream[Text]`, for serving
      The given which lets a `Css` be streamed chunk by chunk, so a large stylesheet is
      never held as one text: a producer writes the rendering from a task while the stream
      is consumed. It needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate` and a `Css.Formatting` in scope, and
      pairs with `.media` to serve a stylesheet as an HTTP response.
  entry .showable   renders a stylesheet as text, under the `Css.Formatting` in scope
      `css.show` writes the whole stylesheet to one `Text`. The rule and declaration layout
      is the formatting's: `formatting.indentedCssFormatting` gives one rule per block with
      two-space indentation and a trailing newline, `formatting.compactCssFormatting` gives
      `a{color:red;}`. Rendering round-trips: a stylesheet read back from its own rendering
      is equal to the original.
  entry .interpolator   the `Interpolable` behind `css"…"`; see `cataclysm.css`
      The contextual-interpolator given which expands a `css"…"` literal. Its result type is
      transparent: a literal of bare declarations is a `Css.Style`, and one with selectors
      or at-rules is a `Css`. Not called directly; the `css` extension on `StringContext`
      is its surface.
  entry .addable   `css + css`: one stylesheet of both rule lists, in order
      The `Addable` given: `css"a { … }" + css"b { … }"` is a stylesheet of both rules, and
      `stylesheet + Css.fontFace(font)` appends a font's at-rules to a page's own styles.
  entry .media   a stylesheet's media type for HTTP: `text/css; charset=UTF-8`
      The `Media` given, so a `Css` serves as a response body with the right content type,
      streamed through `.streamable`.
  entry .Style   a set of declarations for an inline `style="…"` attribute, checked as the code compiles
    detail
        A `Css.Style` is a list of property–value pairs with no selector, for an element's
        `style` attribute. It is built with named arguments, where each name is a camelCase
        property name (converted to kebab-case: `borderWidth` is `border-width`) and each
        value is any type with a `Css.Convertible` whose value-definition type the property
        accepts — a `4.0*Px` for a length, a colour for a colour, `Css.inherit` anywhere:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val red = Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)

        Css.Style(color = red, width = 4.0*Px).text           // t"color: #ff0000; width: 4px"
        Css.Style(borderWidth = 2.0*Px).text                  // t"border-width: 2px"
        Css.Style(color = Css.inherit, width = Css.unset).text
        Css.Style(transitionDuration = 200.0*Ms).text         // t"transition-duration: 200ms"
        Css.Style(color = 4.0*Px)                             // does not compile
        Css.Style(notARealProperty = 4.0*Px)                  // does not compile
        ```

        A property the dataset does not know is a compile error, _cataclysm: `name` is not
        a known CSS property_, and a value of the wrong kind is another, _cataclysm: a
        `length` value is not valid for the `color` property_. A `css"…"` literal of bare
        declarations (`css"color: red; width: 4px"`) is also a `Css.Style`, and two styles
        join with `+`. With `cataclysm.html`, a `Css.Style` is accepted directly as an
        honeycomb `style` attribute (`inlineStyle`); the `style` extension of
        `cataclysm.fonts` makes one which selects a phoenicia `Face`.
    refer cataclysm.css
    refer cataclysm.inlineStyle
    entry .of   a style from already-rendered property–value pairs, unchecked
        `Css.Style.of(List(t"font-family" -> t"\"Inter\""))` builds a style from text pairs
        with no checking; it is the factory the `Css.Style(…)` macro and the font `style`
        extension emit, and the way to carry a declaration the dataset cannot check.
    entry .applyDynamic   `Css.Style()`: the empty style
    entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Css.Style(property = value, …)`: the checked named-argument constructor
        The macro behind `Css.Style(borderWidth = 2.0*Px, color = red)`: each name is read
        as a literal, uncamelled to its CSS property, and the value's `Css.Convertible` is
        summoned; the property must exist in the dataset and must accept a value of the
        convertible's type, or the code does not compile.
    entry .addable   `style + style`: one style of both property lists, in order
    entry #properties   the property–value pairs, each already rendered as text
    entry #text   the declarations as `style` attribute text: `color: #ff0000; width: 4px`
        Each pair rendered as `name: value`, joined with `; ` and no trailing semicolon.
  entry .Value   a rendered CSS value tagged with its value-definition type, `Css.Value of "length"`
    detail
        A `Css.Value` is text already rendered for CSS, whose `Topic` type member names the
        value-definition type it satisfies: `"length"`, `"color"`, `"time"`, `"angle"`,
        `"percentage"`, `"number"`, `"integer"`, `"flex"`, or `"*"` for the CSS-wide
        keywords. The trait is `into`, so a native value with a `Css.Convertible` — a
        quantity, a colour, an `Int` — is accepted wherever a `Css.Value of` its topic is
        expected, lifted by `.converter`. `Css.Value(text)` wraps text with no topic.
    refer cataclysm.Css.Convertible
    entry .apply   wraps already-rendered text as a value with no topic
    entry .converter   the `Conversion` which lifts any `Css.Convertible` type into a `Css.Value of` its topic
        One generic given, so that a new convertible type costs one `Css.Convertible`
        instance and no conversion of its own.
    entry #text   the value as CSS text
  entry .Keyword   the CSS-wide keywords `inherit`, `initial`, `unset`, `revert` and `revert-layer`
      An enum with the cases `Inherit`, `Initial`, `Unset`, `Revert` and `RevertLayer`,
      which are valid as the sole value of every property. They are reached through the
      lowercase values `Css.inherit`, `Css.initial`, `Css.unset`, `Css.revert` and
      `Css.revertLayer`, which avoid the clash a bare `Unset` would have with vacuous's
      `Unset`, and their `Css.Convertible` has the topic `"*"`, so one is accepted for any
      known property in `Css.Style(…)` or `css"…"`.
  entry .ColorKeyword   the colour keywords `transparent` and `currentcolor`
      An enum with the cases `Transparent` and `CurrentColor`, reached as `Css.transparent`
      and `Css.currentColor`, with a `Css.Convertible` of topic `"color"`.
  entry .inherit   the `inherit` keyword, as a value for any property
  entry .initial   the `initial` keyword, as a value for any property
  entry .unset   the `unset` keyword, as a value for any property
      Named in lowercase so that it does not shadow vacuous's `Unset`.
  entry .revert   the `revert` keyword, as a value for any property
  entry .revertLayer   the `revert-layer` keyword, as a value for any property
  entry .transparent   the `transparent` colour keyword
  entry .currentColor   the `currentcolor` colour keyword
  entry .Error   one fault in CSS text, with its line, column and reason
    detail
        Error 251: _invalid CSS at line `n` column `n`: `reason`_, with the reason's own
        number as the subcode. One is raised for each problem found while reading a
        stylesheet or parsing a selector, grammar or value; `read[Css]` collects them all
        into a `Css.Errors`, while `SelectorList.parse` and `SyntaxMatcher.check` raise a
        single `Css.Error` through a `Tactic[Css.Error]`. Constructing one needs a
        `Diagnostics`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        capture[Css.Error](SelectorList.parse(t"a!")).reason
        // Css.Error.Reason.UnexpectedChar('!')
        ```
    refer cataclysm.Css.Errors
    entry .Reason   why the CSS was invalid: the nine kinds of fault, each numbered
      detail
          A `Reason` is a `Clarification` with a `#number`, which becomes the error's subcode:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `UnterminatedComment`  1  _a comment was not terminated_
          row  `UnterminatedString`  2  _a string literal was not terminated_
          row  `UnexpectedEnd`  3  _the input ended before a rule was closed_
          row  `UnexpectedChar(char)`  4  _the character `char` was not expected here_
          row  `EmptySelector`  5  _a selector was expected but none was found_  a rule with nothing before its `{`
          row  `UnknownProperty(name)`  6  _`name` is not a recognized CSS property_
          row  `BadValue(property, value)`  7  _`value` is not a valid value for the `property` property_
          row  `UnsupportedValue(property, types)`  8  _the value of `property` uses an unsupported type_  the property's grammar uses value types the matcher does not implement, listed in `types`
          row  `InvalidName(name)`  9  _`name` is not a valid CSS identifier_  a class or id
          ```
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
      entry #number   the reason's number, 1 to 9, which is the error's subcode
    entry #reason   what was wrong
    entry #line   the line of the fault, as an `Ordinal`
    entry #column   the column of the fault, as an `Ordinal`
  entry .Errors   every `Css.Error` found while reading a stylesheet, raised once
    detail
        _the CSS contained `n` errors_. Raised by `read[Css]` (`Css.aggregable`) after the
        whole stylesheet has been parsed, holding each fault in `#errors` in document
        order, so a stylesheet with several bad declarations reports all of them at once.
        The type is plural because reporting one fault at a time from a document which has
        several is the wrong shape for a linter or a build step.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        capture[Css.Errors](t"a { colour: red; bogus: 1px }".read[Css]).errors.size   // 2
        ```
    entry #errors   the faults, in document order
    entry #+   this aggregate with one more error appended
  entry .Convertible   which native types render as CSS values, and of which value-definition type
    detail
        `T is Css.Convertible of "length"` says that a `T` renders to CSS text (`#value`)
        satisfying the value-definition type `<length>`. It is the typeclass behind every
        substitution in `css"…"` and every argument to `Css.Style(…)`: the macro summons the
        instance for the value's own type, reads its `Topic`, and checks that the property
        accepts a value of that type, so `css"a { color: $length }"` does not compile. One
        generic `Conversion` (`Css.Value.converter`) lifts any convertible type into a
        `Css.Value of Topic`, so a new type costs one instance here.

        The instances in the companion cover the CSS units — every `Quantity` in the
        `Pixels`, `Rems`, `Exs`, `Chs`, viewport and percentage units, and quantitative's
        own `Ems`, `Metres`, `Inches`, `Points`, `Picas` and `Seconds` — the angle and flex
        units, `Int` and `Double`, the keywords, and any colour with a `Chromatic` instance.
        A number renders without a redundant `.0` (`12px`, not `12.0px`), rounded to six
        decimal places so a converted physical length does not leak floating-point noise.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Swatch(hue: Int, saturation: Int, lightness: Int)

        given Swatch is Css.Convertible of "color" = swatch =>
          t"hsl(${swatch.hue}deg ${swatch.saturation}% ${swatch.lightness}%)"

        Css.Style(color = Swatch(200, 50, 50)).text    // t"color: hsl(200deg 50% 50%)"
        ```

        A user-defined instance's topic must be one the property's grammar names:
        `"length"`, `"color"`, `"percentage"`, `"number"`, `"integer"`, `"time"`,
        `"angle"` or `"flex"` are probed against the grammar; any other topic is accepted
        unchecked.
    refer cataclysm.Css.Value
    refer cataclysm.Px
    entry .pixels   a `Pixels` quantity as `<length>`: `4px`
    entry .ems   an `Ems` quantity (quantitative's) as `<length>`: `2em`
    entry .rems   a `Rems` quantity as `<length>`: `2rem`
    entry .exs   an `Exs` quantity as `<length>`: `1ex`
    entry .chs   a `Chs` quantity as `<length>`: `40ch`
    entry .vws   a `ViewportWidths` quantity as `<length>`: `100vw`
    entry .vhs   a `ViewportHeights` quantity as `<length>`: `100vh`
    entry .vmins   a `ViewportMins` quantity as `<length>`: `50vmin`
    entry .vmaxes   a `ViewportMaxes` quantity as `<length>`: `50vmax`
    entry .metres   any physical length (a `Metres` quantity) as `<length>`, in `mm`
        A `Distance` quantity — written as `3.0*Cm`, `10.0*Mm`, `1.0*Metre` or any
        quantitative length — renders in millimetres, since CSS reads `cm`, `mm` and `m`
        alike: `3.0*Cm` is `30mm`. Inches, points and picas keep their own units.
    entry .inches   an `Inches` quantity as `<length>`: `1in`
    entry .points   a `Points` quantity as `<length>`: `12pt`
    entry .picas   a `Picas` quantity as `<length>`: `1pc`
    entry .percents   a `Percents` quantity as `<percentage>`: `50%`
    entry .chromatic   any colour with a `Chromatic` instance as `<color>`, rendered as hex
        Covers anticipation's `Chroma` and every iridescence colour form (`Srgb`, `Hsl`,
        `Cielab`, …, and the `rgb"#3344ff"` literal) through their `Color.chromatic`
        instance, each rendered as a six-digit hex triplet from its red, green and blue channels, clamped
        to 0–255. The instance is sought for any supertype of the colour, so a colour
        class which gets its `Chromatic` from its form is found when `Css.Style(…)` asks
        for the value's exact type.
    entry .seconds   any time (a `Seconds` quantity) as `<time>`, in `ms`
        `200.0*Ms` is `200ms`; `1.5*S` is `1500ms`.
    entry .degrees   a `Degrees` quantity as `<angle>`: `90deg`
    entry .radians   a `Radians` quantity as `<angle>`: `1rad`
    entry .turns   a `Turns` quantity as `<angle>`: `0.5turn`
    entry .flexes   a `Flexes` quantity as `<flex>`: `2fr`
    entry .integer   an `Int` as `<integer>`
    entry .decimal   a `Double` as `<number>`, without a redundant `.0`
    entry .keyword   a `Css.Keyword` as its CSS spelling, valid for every property
        The topic is `"*"`, which the property check accepts for any known property.
    entry .colorKeyword   a `Css.ColorKeyword` as `transparent` or `currentcolor`
    entry #value   renders a native value as CSS text
  entry .Syntax   a property's value grammar, in CSS value-definition syntax, as a tree
    detail
        The parsed form of a value-definition-syntax string such as `<line-width> ||
        <line-style> || <color>` — the notation in which every property's permitted values
        are described in the MDN dataset — which `SyntaxMatcher` matches a concrete value
        against. `PropertyDef#grammar` is the `Css.Syntax` of a property.

        The cases follow the notation. `Keyword(name)` is a literal identifier such as
        `auto`; `Literal(token)` a literal token such as `/`, `,` or a quoted `'+'`;
        `Type(name, bounds)` a `<length>` or `<integer [1,4]>`, with the bounds kept raw;
        `Property(name)` a reference to another property's grammar, `<'border-width'>`;
        `Function(name, body)` a functional notation, `rgb( <number>#{3} )`;
        `Sequence(terms)` juxtaposition, in order; `OneOf(options)` the `|` combinator
        (exactly one); `AnyOf(terms)` the `||` combinator (one or more, in any order);
        `AllOf(terms)` the `&&` combinator (all, in any order); `Repeated(term, min, max,
        separated)` the multipliers `?`, `*`, `+`, `{m,n}` and `#` (comma-separated), with
        `max` `Unset` for unbounded; and `Mandatory(term)` the `!` multiplier.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val grammar = Css.Syntax.OneOf(List(Css.Syntax.Keyword(t"auto"), Css.Syntax.Type(t"length", Unset)))
        ```

        It lives under `Css` rather than at the top level so that it does not clash with
        stenography's `Syntax` in the umbrella.
    refer cataclysm.SyntaxMatcher
    refer cataclysm.PropertyDef
  entry #rules   the stylesheet's nodes, in order

entry .css   the `css"…"` interpolator: a stylesheet or inline style, checked as the code compiles
    `css"a { color: red }"` is a `Css`, and `css"color: red; width: 4px"` — bare
    declarations with no selector — is a `Css.Style`; the result type is transparent, and
    decided by the content. The literal is parsed and validated while the code compiles,
    with the same checks as `read[Css]`: an unknown property, a value which does not
    satisfy the property's grammar, an unterminated comment or string, or a malformed
    selector is a compile error, reported at the line and column of the fault.

    Substitutions are typed. In a **value** position, `$value` must be the whole value of a
    declaration, and its type must have a `Css.Convertible` whose topic the property
    accepts: a colour for `color`, a length for `width`, a time for `transition-duration`,
    or a CSS-wide keyword for anything. In a **selector** position, `$name` must be a
    `Name[CssClass]` or a `Name[DomId]`, which render as a class selector (dot and name) or an id selector (hash and name) and may be
    compounded with literal text (`div$button`). Anywhere else — a property name, an
    at-rule prelude, or mixed with literal text inside a value (`margin: $width 0`) — is a
    compile error.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A literal stylesheet, checked as it compiles
      code
          import soundness.*

          css"a { color: red }"
    frame
      label A colour and a length substituted into values
      code
          import soundness.*

          val red = Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
          val width = 4.0*Px

          css"a { color: $red; width: $width }"       // a { color: #ff0000; width: 4px }
    frame
      label A class name substituted into the selector
      code
          import soundness.*

          val red = Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
          val width = 4.0*Px
          val button = Name[CssClass](t"button")

          css"div$button { color: $red; width: $width }"   // div.button { color: #ff0000; width: 4px }
    frame
      label A wrong-typed substitution does not compile
      code
          import soundness.*

          val red = Srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
          val width = 4.0*Px
          val button = Name[CssClass](t"button")

          css"div$button { color: $red; width: $width }"
          css"a { color: $width }"                     // does not compile
    ##
    ```

    The result is a `Css` built from the parsed nodes, with each substitution rendered
    through its `Css.Convertible` at runtime, so `css"a { color: $red }"` is equal to
    `t"a { color: #ff0000 }".read[Css]`. Stylesheets join with `+`, as do inline styles.

entry .classes   the class names a stylesheet's selectors mention, as a `Set[Name[CssClass]]`
    An extension on `Css`. Walks every rule, including rules nested in other rules and in
    at-rule bodies, and the selector-list arguments of `:is()`, `:where()`, `:not()`,
    `:has()` and `:nth-child(… of …)`, collecting each `Simple.Class`. This is the set
    `Styles` and `cssBindings.checkedBinding` check an HTML template's class names
    against.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    t".a #b { color: red } @media screen { .c:not(.d) e#f { color: blue } }".read[Css].classes
    // the names a, c and d
    ```

entry .ids   the id names a stylesheet's selectors mention, as a `Set[Name[DomId]]`
    An extension on `Css`, the counterpart of `classes` for `Simple.Id` parts: every
    id selector in every selector list, however deeply nested.

entry .formatting   the two standard renderings of a stylesheet, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Rendering a `Css` needs a `Css.Formatting` in scope, and `import soundness.*` brings
      none in: a program imports one of these by name, or binds its own
      `Css.Formatting(newlines, spaces)` as a local given.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Indented, for reading
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

            val css = t"a { color: red; width: 4px }".read[Css]

            locally:
              import formatting.indentedCssFormatting
              css.show        // t"a {\n  color: red;\n  width: 4px;\n}\n"
      frame
        label Compact, for serving
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

            val css = t"a { color: red; width: 4px }".read[Css]

            locally:
              import formatting.compactCssFormatting
              css.show        // t"a{color:red;width:4px;}"
      ##
      ```
  entry .indentedCssFormatting   one rule per block, declarations on indented lines, a trailing newline
      `Css.Formatting(newlines = true, spaces = true)`: a space before each `{` and after
      each `:`, two-space indentation per level, a blank line between top-level rules, and
      a final newline.
  entry .compactCssFormatting   no newlines and no cosmetic spaces: `a{color:red;}`
      `Css.Formatting(newlines = false, spaces = false)`. A `;` still follows every
      declaration, including the last in a block.

entry .construables   the media-type construction for stylesheets, a given to import by name
  detail
      The `Construable` package, which other libraries also populate: a given here lets a
      body of a given media type be constructed as a value.
  entry .cssConstruable   constructs a `Css` from a `text/css` body
      `"text/css" is Construable to Css`: an HTTP body or other media-typed source of CSS
      text is read as a `Css`, through `Css.aggregable`.

entry .SelectorList   a comma-separated list of selectors, the prelude of a rule
  detail
      The loosest of the three levels of a CSS selector: a `SelectorList` holds the
      alternatives separated by commas, each a `Selector` (a complex selector: compounds
      joined by combinators), each of whose `Compound`s is a run of `Simple` parts. It is
      what a `Css.Node.Rule` carries, and what `:is()`, `:not()`, `:has()` and `:where()`
      take as their argument.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val list: SelectorList = SelectorList.parse(t"ul > li.item:nth-child(2n+1), a[href^=\"http\" i]")
      list.selectors.size     // 2
      list.show               // t"ul > li.item:nth-child(2n+1), a[href^=http i]"
      ```

      The parser follows Selectors Level 4: all five combinators including the column
      `||`, namespaced type and attribute selectors, every attribute matcher and the `i`/`s`
      modifier, functional pseudo-classes with nested selector lists, `An+B` arguments with
      `odd`, `even` and an `of` clause, and the nesting selector `&`. A malformed selector
      raises a `Css.Error`.
  refer cataclysm.Selector
  refer cataclysm.Compound
  refer cataclysm.Simple
  entry .showable   renders the list with `, ` between selectors
  entry .focusable   lets a selector list be a tarantula focus, with the strategy `css selector`
      The `Focusable` given, so a browser-automation lookup can take a parsed selector
      rather than text.
  entry .parse   parses selector text, raising a `Css.Error` if it is malformed
      Needs a `Tactic[Css.Error]`. The text is taken as comment-free and
      whitespace-normalized, as a rule prelude is after `read[Css]` has stripped comments;
      trailing input after a complete selector list is `UnexpectedChar`, and an empty list
      is `EmptySelector`.
  entry .unsafeRead   the parse the `css"…"` expansion uses, gated by `Unsafe`
      Rebuilds a rule's selector at runtime from text the interpolator already parsed at
      expansion, so it cannot fail; the `Unsafe` token asserts that. Arbitrary text goes
      through `.parse`.
  entry #selectors   the alternatives, in order

entry .Selector   a complex selector: a head compound and the combinator–compound steps after it
  detail
      `Selector(lead, head, rest)` is a selector such as `ul > li a`: its `#head` is the
      first `Compound` (`ul`), and `#rest` the steps which follow, each a `Combinator` and
      the `Compound` it reaches (`Child` to `li`, `Descendant` to `a`). `#lead` is set only
      for a relative selector, the `>` in `:has(> img)`, and is otherwise `Unset`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ul = Compound(List(Simple.Type(Unset, t"ul")))
      val li = Compound(List(Simple.Type(Unset, t"li"), Simple.Class(Name[CssClass](t"item"))))
      val selector = Selector(Unset, ul, List((Combinator.Child, li)))
      selector.show     // t"ul > li.item"
      ```
  refer cataclysm.SelectorList
  refer cataclysm.Combinator
  entry .showable   renders the selector, with a space around every combinator but the descendant's
  entry #lead   the leading combinator of a relative selector, or `Unset`
  entry #head   the first compound
  entry #rest   the steps after the head, each a combinator and the compound it reaches

entry .Compound   a compound selector: simple selectors with nothing between them, `li.item:hover`
  detail
      A run of `Simple` parts bound together with no combinator and no whitespace: a type
      or universal selector, if any, followed by ids, classes, attribute selectors,
      pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements. `Compound(List(Simple.Type(Unset, t"a"),
      Simple.PseudoClass(t"hover", Unset)))` is `a:hover`.
  entry .showable   renders the parts concatenated
  entry #parts   the simple selectors, in order

entry .Simple   one simple selector: a type, class, id, attribute, pseudo-class, pseudo-element, `*` or `&`
  detail
      The eight kinds of simple selector, as an enum. `Universal(namespace)` is `*`, with
      an optional namespace `Prefix`; `Type(namespace, name)` an element name such as `div`
      or `svg|rect`; `Id(name)` an id selector (hash and name), typed as a `Name[DomId]`; `Class(name)` a
      a class selector (dot and name), typed as a `Name[CssClass]`; `Nesting` the `&` of a nested rule;
      `Attribute(namespace, name, test)` a `[name]` with an optional `AttributeTest` such as
      `^="https" i`; `PseudoClass(name, argument)` a `:hover` or `:nth-child(2n+1)`, with an
      optional `PseudoArgument`; and `PseudoElement(name, argument)` a `::before`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Simple.Class(Name[CssClass](t"button")).show                         // t".button"
      Simple.Attribute(Unset, t"href", AttributeTest(AttributeMatcher.Prefix, t"https", 'i')).show
      // t"[href^=https i]"
      ```

      Class and id names are `Name`s, so a name which is not a valid CSS identifier is
      rejected when parsed (`Css.Error.Reason.InvalidName`), and `classes` and `ids` on a
      `Css` return them typed.
  refer cataclysm.PseudoArgument
  refer cataclysm.AttributeTest
  refer cataclysm.Prefix
  entry .showable   renders each kind as CSS: `*`, `div`, an id or class selector, `&`, `[a^=b i]`, `:is(…)`, `::before`
      An `An+B` argument is canonicalized: `1n` renders as `n`, `-1n` as `-n`, and an `a`
      of zero as the bare `b`. Attribute values render unquoted.

entry .Combinator   how two compounds relate: descendant, child, next sibling, subsequent sibling or column
    An enum of the five combinators of Selectors Level 4: `Descendant` (whitespace),
    `Child` (`>`), `NextSibling` (`+`), `SubsequentSibling` (`~`) and `Column` (`||`). A
    `Selector#rest` pairs one with each compound after the head. The `showable` given in
    the companion renders each as its symbol, with the descendant as a single space.

entry .AttributeMatcher   how an attribute selector compares its value: `=`, `~=`, `|=`, `^=`, `$=` or `*=`
    An enum of the six matchers: `Exact` (`=`), `Includes` (`~=`, a whitespace-separated
    word), `DashMatch` (`|=`, the value or a `value-` prefix), `Prefix` (`^=`), `Suffix`
    (`$=`) and `Substring` (`*=`). An `AttributeTest` holds one with the value it compares.

entry .AttributeTest   the right-hand side of an attribute selector: matcher, value and modifier
  detail
      `AttributeTest(matcher, value, modifier)` is the `^="https" i` of `[href^="https"
      i]`: the `AttributeMatcher`, the value compared (unquoted), and the optional
      case-sensitivity modifier `i` or `s`. A `Simple.Attribute` with no test, `[disabled]`,
      holds `Unset` instead.
  entry #matcher   how the value is compared
  entry #value   the value compared, without its quotes
  entry #modifier   the `i` or `s` case modifier, or `Unset`

entry .Prefix   the namespace prefix of a type, universal or attribute selector
    An enum of the three forms, which mean different things and are kept distinct: `Any`
    is `*|` (any namespace), `Default` is a bare `|` (no namespace), and `Named(prefix)` is
    a declared namespace, `svg|`. A selector with no prefix at all holds `Unset`.

entry .PseudoArgument   the argument of a functional pseudo-class: a selector list, an `An+B`, or raw text
    An enum of the three shapes. `Selectors(list)` is the nested `SelectorList` of `:is()`,
    `:where()`, `:not()` and `:has()`, so their contents are themselves inspectable;
    `Nth(a, b, of)` is the `An+B` of `:nth-child()` and its siblings, with `odd` normalized
    to `2n+1` and `even` to `2n`, and the optional `of` selector list kept alongside; and
    `Raw(text)` is the argument of any other function, such as `:lang(en)`, kept as text.

entry .PropertyDef   the definition of a CSS property from the MDN dataset: its name and value grammar
  detail
      Every known CSS property — over six hundred, from the MDN `css/properties.json`
      dataset (CC0), compiled into the library at build time — is a `PropertyDef` of its
      `#name` and its `#syntax`, the raw value-definition-syntax string, which `#grammar`
      parses on demand into a `Css.Syntax`. `PropertyDef.of(name)` looks one up; `.all` is
      the whole table and `.list` the same in dataset order.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val width: PropertyDef = PropertyDef.of(t"width").or(halt(t"unknown"))
      width.syntax                                      // the dataset's grammar text
      SyntaxMatcher.check(width, t"4px")                // Outcome.Valid
      SyntaxMatcher.check(width, t"wide")               // Outcome.Invalid
      PropertyDef.of(t"colour")                         // Unset
      ```

      Custom properties (`--name`) are valid without a definition and are the caller's
      responsibility: `of` returns `Unset` for them.
  refer cataclysm.SyntaxMatcher
  refer cataclysm.Css.Syntax
  entry .list   every known property, in dataset order
  entry .all   every known property, keyed by name in a `Dictionary`
  entry .of   the definition of a named property, or `Unset` if it is not a recognized property
  entry #name   the property's name, in kebab-case
  entry #syntax   the value grammar as the dataset writes it, `<length> | auto`
  entry #grammar   the value grammar parsed to a `Css.Syntax`, computed on first use

entry .SyntaxMatcher   checks a property value against its grammar: valid, invalid or unsupported
  detail
      `SyntaxMatcher.check(property, value)` tokenizes the value (`ValueToken`) and
      matches it, with backtracking, against the property's `Css.Syntax`, resolving
      composite `<type>`s from the MDN `syntaxes.json` dataset and matching the leaf
      primitives — `<length>`, `<percentage>`, `<number>`, `<integer>`, `<angle>`,
      `<time>`, `<resolution>`, `<frequency>`, `<flex>`, `<ratio>`, `<string>`, `<url>`,
      `<hex-color>`, the identifier types — at the token level. The result is an
      `Outcome`: `Valid`, `Invalid`, or `Unsupported(types)` when the grammar reaches a
      type the matcher implements neither as a composite nor as a primitive, so an
      unimplemented corner of the grammar is reported rather than passed or failed
      silently. This is the check `read[Css]`, `css"…"` and `Css.Style(…)` apply to every
      declaration.

      A value containing an arbitrary-substitution function, `var()` or `env()`, is always
      valid, as a pending-substitution value is; the CSS-wide keywords are valid as the
      sole value of every property; and `calc()`, `min()`, `max()`, `clamp()` and the
      other math functions are accepted wherever a numeric primitive is expected.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val grammar = Css.Syntax.Repeated(Css.Syntax.Type(t"length", Unset), 1, Unset, true)
      SyntaxMatcher.check(grammar, t"1px, 2px, 3px")     // Outcome.Valid
      SyntaxMatcher.check(grammar, t"1px 2px")           // Outcome.Invalid
      ```
  refer cataclysm.PropertyDef
  refer cataclysm.Outcome
  refer cataclysm.ValueToken
  entry .check   a value against a property's grammar, or against a `Css.Syntax` directly
      Two overloads: `check(property: PropertyDef, value)` uses the property's parsed
      grammar, and `check(grammar: Css.Syntax, value)` any grammar. Both need a
      `Tactic[Css.Error]`, raised only when the value's text cannot be tokenized (an
      unterminated string).

entry .Outcome   the result of checking a value against a grammar: `Valid`, `Invalid` or `Unsupported`
    An enum returned by `SyntaxMatcher.check`. `Valid` and `Invalid` are a definite
    verdict; `Unsupported(types)` names the value-definition types the matcher could not
    verify against, which `read[Css]` reports as `Css.Error.Reason.UnsupportedValue`.

entry .ValueToken   one token of a property value: identifier, number, dimension, string, function, …
    The unit a property value is broken into before matching, following the CSS Syntax
    Module Level 3 tokenizer for the tokens which appear in values. `SyntaxMatcher` consumes
    them; they are exposed so that a tool can inspect a value's lexical shape. The cases:

    ```tabular
    column  Case
    column  Token
    row  `Whitespace`  whitespace
    row  `Ident(value)`  an identifier: `auto`, `solid` or `--my-var`
    row  `Function(name)`  an identifier directly followed by `(`
    row  `Hash(value)`  `#ff0000`, holding `ff0000`
    row  `Quoted(value)`  a string's contents, without its quotes
    row  `Url(value)`  an unquoted `url(…)`
    row  `Number(value, integer, raw)`  a number
    row  `Percentage(value, raw)`  a percentage
    row  `Dimension(value, unit, raw)`  a number with a unit: `10px`
    row  `Comma`  `,`
    row  `Open`, `Close`  `(` and `)`
    row  `Delim(char)`  any other single character, such as `/` or `+`
    ```

entry .Px   one CSS pixel, `Quantity[Pixels[1]]`: a length is `4.0*Px`
    The unit value which a number multiplies to make a length in pixels, rendered as `4px`
    through `Css.Convertible.pixels`. `Px` is a fresh dimension with no ratio to any
    other, so `2.0*Px + 3.0*Cm` and `(1.0*Px).convert[Metres]` do not compile: a pixel's
    physical size is the browser's to decide. The same holds for every relative and
    viewport unit (`Rem`, `Ex`, `Ch`, `Vw`, `Vh`, `Vmin`, `Vmax`, `Pct`), the angles
    (`Deg`, `Rad`, `Turn`) and grid flex (`Fr`); the physical units `Cm`, `Mm`, `Pt`, `Pc`
    and quantitative's own `Inch` and `Metre` share the `Distance` dimension and
    interconvert as usual, and `S` and `Ms` are quantitative seconds.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val width = 4.0*Px
    val sum = 2.0*Px + 3.0*Px        // 5px
    2.0*Px + 1.0*Vh                  // does not compile
    ```

entry .Rem   one root-element font size, `Quantity[Rems[1]]`: `2.0*Rem` renders as `2rem`
entry .Ex   one x-height of the element's font, `Quantity[Exs[1]]`: renders as `ex`
entry .Ch   one advance of the element's font's `0`, `Quantity[Chs[1]]`: renders as `ch`
entry .Vw   one per cent of the viewport's width, `Quantity[ViewportWidths[1]]`: renders as `vw`
entry .Vh   one per cent of the viewport's height, `Quantity[ViewportHeights[1]]`: renders as `vh`
entry .Vmin   one per cent of the viewport's smaller side, `Quantity[ViewportMins[1]]`: renders as `vmin`
entry .Vmax   one per cent of the viewport's larger side, `Quantity[ViewportMaxes[1]]`: renders as `vmax`
entry .Cm   one centimetre, as quantitative `Metres`: `3.0*Cm` renders as `30mm`
    A convenient magnitude of `Metres`, not a new type: `3.0*Cm == 0.03*Metre`, and every
    physical length renders in millimetres through `Css.Convertible.metres`.
entry .Mm   one millimetre, as quantitative `Metres`: `10.0*Mm` renders as `10mm`
entry .Pt   one typographic point, `Quantity[Points[1]]`: renders as `pt`
    Quantitative's `Points`, so it interconverts with inches, picas and metres.
entry .Pc   one pica, `Quantity[Picas[1]]`: renders as `pc`
entry .Pct   one per cent, `Quantity[Percents[1]]`: `50.0*Pct` renders as `50%`
    A fresh dimension, not a number: a percentage cannot be added to a length or a
    `Double`, and `Css.Convertible.percents` renders it with the topic `"percentage"`.
entry .S   one second, as quantitative `Seconds`: `1.5*S` renders as `1500ms`
    Times render in milliseconds whatever the magnitude they were written in.
entry .Ms   one millisecond, as quantitative `Seconds`: `200.0*Ms` renders as `200ms`
entry .Deg   one degree, `Quantity[Degrees[1]]`: `90.0*Deg` renders as `90deg`
    Angles are fresh, mutually inconvertible dimensions: `1.0*Deg + 1.0*Rad` does not
    compile.
entry .Rad   one radian, `Quantity[Radians[1]]`: renders as `rad`
entry .Turn   one full turn, `Quantity[Turns[1]]`: `0.5*Turn` renders as `0.5turn`
entry .Fr   one fraction of a grid track's free space, `Quantity[Flexes[1]]`: `2.0*Fr` renders as `2fr`

entry .Pixels   the units of CSS pixels, `Pixels[1]`, over the `CssPixel` dimension
    The units type of `Px`; `Quantity[Pixels[1]]` is a length in pixels. The `Power` type
    parameter is the exponent, as in every quantitative units type.
entry .Rems   the units of root-element font sizes, over `CssRootFontSize`
entry .Exs   the units of x-heights, over `CssXHeight`
entry .Chs   the units of character advances, over `CssCharacterWidth`
entry .ViewportWidths   the units of viewport-width percentages, over `CssViewportWidth`
entry .ViewportHeights   the units of viewport-height percentages, over `CssViewportHeight`
entry .ViewportMins   the units of viewport-minimum percentages, over `CssViewportMin`
entry .ViewportMaxes   the units of viewport-maximum percentages, over `CssViewportMax`
entry .Percents   the units of percentages, over `CssRatio`
entry .Degrees   the units of degrees, over `CssDegree`
entry .Radians   the units of radians, over `CssRadian`
entry .Turns   the units of turns, over `CssTurn`
entry .Flexes   the units of grid flex fractions, over `CssFlex`

entry .CssPixel   the dimension of CSS pixels, with no ratio to any other
    A sealed dimension of its own, so that a pixel length can neither be added to nor
    converted into any physical or relative length. Each of the CSS-specific dimensions —
    `CssPixel`, `CssRootFontSize`, `CssXHeight`, `CssCharacterWidth`, `CssViewportWidth`,
    `CssViewportHeight`, `CssViewportMin`, `CssViewportMax`, `CssRatio`, `CssDegree`,
    `CssRadian`, `CssTurn` and `CssFlex` — is deliberately inconvertible, since its
    physical size is decided by the browser, the font or the viewport, not the stylesheet.
entry .CssRootFontSize   the dimension of `rem` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssXHeight   the dimension of `ex` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssCharacterWidth   the dimension of `ch` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssViewportWidth   the dimension of `vw` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssViewportHeight   the dimension of `vh` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssViewportMin   the dimension of `vmin` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssViewportMax   the dimension of `vmax` lengths, inconvertible
entry .CssRatio   the dimension of percentages, inconvertible to a plain number
entry .CssDegree   the dimension of degree angles, inconvertible to radians or turns
entry .CssRadian   the dimension of radian angles, inconvertible
entry .CssTurn   the dimension of turn angles, inconvertible
entry .CssFlex   the dimension of grid flex fractions, inconvertible

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `css"…"` and `Css.Style(…)`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
  entry .expand   the macro behind `css"…"`: parses the literal at expansion and rebuilds it as a `Css` or `Css.Style`
      Joins the literal parts with a sentinel at each substitution, parses and validates
      the whole at compile time (a fault is reported at its line and column), and splices
      the nodes back as an expression: a declaration value which is a lone substitution
      becomes the typed value, checked against the property through `propertyIssue`; a
      substitution in a selector must be a `Name[CssClass]` or a `Name[DomId]`.
  entry .style   the macro behind `Css.Style(property = value, …)`
      Uncamels each literal name to its property, summons the value's `Css.Convertible`,
      checks the property accepts the convertible's topic, and emits `Css.Style.of`.
  entry .topicOf   the value-definition topic a `Css.Convertible` instance's type carries, or empty
  entry .propertyIssue   `Unset` if a property accepts a value of a topic; otherwise the message saying why not
      An unknown property, or a sample of the topic (`1px` for `"length"`, `#000000` for
      `"color"`, …) which the property's grammar rejects. The topic `"*"` is accepted for
      every known property, and a topic with no sample is not checked.

entry .Web   the web as a typesetting medium: how a browser obtains a typeface, and the generic families
  detail
      `Web` is a phoenicia `Medium`: the medium of text set by a browser from CSS, where a
      typeface is available if the page carries a `@font-face` for it, imports a
      stylesheet which does, or names a generic family. A typeface is provided for the web
      by a given `(Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Web`, declared once through one
      of the companion's factories — `.linked` for a font file at a URL, `.imported` for a
      font service's stylesheet, `.local` for a font the reader has installed — or `in
      Medium` through phoenicia's `Typesettable.embedded` for a file carried in the page.
      The six CSS generic families (`.sansSerif`, `.serif`, `.monospace`, `.cursive`,
      `.fantasy`, `.systemUi`) are provided here already, in the medium's companion, which
      is in the implicit scope of `(Typeface of "sans-serif") is Typesettable in Web`, so
      they need no declaration and no import.

      A `Font in Web` is a face paired with the provision in scope for its typeface, made
      with `.font` (or phoenicia's `Font.of[Web](face)`); a typeface with no provision in
      scope cannot be a web font, and a face the provision's coverage cannot set — an
      italic from a file declared upright-only — is a `Font.Error` at the pairing. The
      font's at-rules are then `Css.fontFace(font)`, and the declarations which select the
      face are `font.style`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A typeface, provided for the web as a linked font file
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import formatting.compactCssFormatting

            val inter = Typeface["Inter"]

            given (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Web =
              Web.linked(url"https://fonts.example/inter.woff2",
                Coverage(weights = Weight.Thin to Weight.Black, italic = true),
                Typesettable.Source.Format.Woff2)
      frame
        label The face paired with its provision: a font for the web
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import formatting.compactCssFormatting

            val inter = Typeface["Inter"]

            given (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Web =
              Web.linked(url"https://fonts.example/inter.woff2",
                Coverage(weights = Weight.Thin to Weight.Black, italic = true),
                Typesettable.Source.Format.Woff2)

            val heading: Font in Web = Web.font(inter.bold.italic)
      frame
        label The at-rule the page needs, and the declarations which select the face
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import formatting.compactCssFormatting

            val inter = Typeface["Inter"]

            given (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Web =
              Web.linked(url"https://fonts.example/inter.woff2",
                Coverage(weights = Weight.Thin to Weight.Black, italic = true),
                Typesettable.Source.Format.Woff2)

            val heading: Font in Web = Web.font(inter.bold.italic)

            Css.fontFace(heading).show
            // @font-face{font-family:"Inter";src:url("https://fonts.example/inter.woff2") format("woff2");font-weight:100 900;font-style:italic;}
            heading.style.text
            // font-family: "Inter"; font-weight: 700; font-style: italic
      ##
      ```

      A named given's type is parsed with the restricted given-type grammar, in which an
      infix operand cannot be a literal type, so the typeface is parenthesised:
      `given inter: (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Web = …`. A provision which
      renderers other than the browser can also use — an SVG's `<style>`, a PDF — asks
      for `Typesettable in (? >: Web)`, which a `Web` provision and an embedded `Medium`
      provision both satisfy.
  refer cataclysm.fontFace
  refer cataclysm.FontFace
  refer cataclysm.style
  entry .Provision   a web provision as a value: its sources and declared coverage
    detail
        The class every factory in `Web` returns, so that a provision has a stable type:
        `Web.Provision[family](sources, coverage)` is a `(Typeface of family) is
        Typesettable in Web` whose `#sources` are the `Typesettable.Source`s the browser
        obtains the typeface from and whose `#coverage` is the `Coverage` it was declared
        with. `FontFace.rules` renders one source at a time.
    entry #sources   where the browser gets the typeface: linked, imported, local or generic
    entry #coverage   the weights, slants, widths and axes the provision was declared to offer
    entry #Self   the typeface provided, `Typeface of family`
    entry #Form   the medium, `Web`
  entry .sansSerif   the generic `sans-serif` family, provided on the web with no declaration
      A browser sets each generic family from whatever it has, so the provision has a
      single `Generic` source and `Coverage.Unknown`, which admits every face; it renders
      no at-rule, and `font-family: sans-serif` unquoted.
  entry .serif   the generic `serif` family, provided with no declaration
  entry .monospace   the generic `monospace` family, provided with no declaration
  entry .cursive   the generic `cursive` family, provided with no declaration
  entry .fantasy   the generic `fantasy` family, provided with no declaration
  entry .systemUi   the generic `system-ui` family, provided with no declaration
  entry .linked   a provision from a font file the browser fetches from a URL
      `Web.linked[family](url, coverage, format)` takes any URL type `Abstractable across
      Urls to Text` (an `HttpUrl` from `url"…"`, or text), the `Coverage` the file is
      declared to have — since the file is not to hand to be read, only what is declared
      is admitted, so a font declared at one weight refuses a request for another rather
      than letting the browser synthesize it — and an optional
      `Typesettable.Source.Format` (`Ttf`, `Otf`, `Woff` or `Woff2`) for the `format()`
      hint. The `family` type argument is normally inferred from the given's declared
      type. It renders as a `@font-face` whose `src` is `url("…") format("woff2")`.
  entry .local   a provision from a font the reader is expected to have installed
      `Web.local[family](coverage)` names the typeface in a `src: local("Name")`, the
      typeface's family name being the `family` literal. Nothing guarantees the reader has
      it, so this is the weakest provision, and its coverage — `Coverage.Unknown` unless
      given, which admits every face — says only what is declared.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.compactCssFormatting

      given (Typeface of "Menlo") is Typesettable in Web = Web.local()
      Css.fontFace(Web.font(Typeface["Menlo"].face)).show
      // @font-face{font-family:"Menlo";src:local("Menlo");}
      ```
  entry .imported   a provision from a stylesheet, such as a font service's, which declares the typeface itself
      `Web.imported[family](url, coverage)` takes the stylesheet's URL (any type
      `Abstractable across Urls to Text`) and the coverage it is declared to offer; the
      page then carries `@import url("…");`, which `Css.fontFaces` places before every
      other rule, as CSS requires.
  entry .sansSerifFont   the generic sans-serif as a `Font in Web`: a default which cannot fail
      `Typeface.SansSerif.face` paired with `.sansSerif`; since a generic family admits
      every face, the pairing never raises, and `Css.fontFace` of it is empty.
  entry .font   a face paired with its provision in scope, as a `Font in Web`
      `Web.font(face)` needs a `(Typeface of family) is Typesettable in (? >: Web)` given
      for the face's typeface — a web provision or an embedded one — and a
      `Tactic[Font.Error]`, raised when the provision's coverage cannot set the face. A
      typeface with no provision in scope is a compile error. The face is an inline
      parameter, so a face whose request is in its type (`inter.bold.italic`) and a
      provision whose font file is on the classpath are checked as the code compiles,
      with the same refusal reported as a compile error. Where the expected type already
      names the medium, phoenicia's `Font(face)` suffices.

entry .FontFace   the `@font-face` and `@import` rules a font's provision needs, as `Css.Node`s
  detail
      Renders a provision's sources as at-rules: an embedded font becomes a `@font-face`
      whose `src` is a base-64 `data:` URI (`font/otf` with `format("opentype")` for an
      `Opentype`, `font/ttf` with `format("truetype")` otherwise), so the stylesheet — or
      the SVG carrying it — is self-contained; a linked font a `@font-face` with its URL
      and format hint; a local font a `@font-face` with `src: local("Name")`; an imported
      stylesheet an `@import url("…");`; and a generic family nothing. Each `@font-face`
      carries `font-weight` (a single weight or a `100 900` range), and `font-style` and
      `font-stretch` where the coverage's faces differ from the defaults, spanning every
      entry of the coverage so that a browser's face matching finds what the file has.
      `Css.fontFace` and `Css.fontFaces` are the usual surface.
  refer cataclysm.fontFace
  refer cataclysm.Web
  entry .rules   the at-rules for one font's typeface, as nodes
  entry .stylesheet   a stylesheet of the rules for several fonts, each typeface once, `@import`s first
      Takes a `List` of fonts; a typeface which appears more than once gets its rules once,
      and every `@import` is moved before the `@font-face`s, as CSS requires.

entry .fontFace   `Css.fontFace(font)`: the at-rules a page needs for a font's typeface, as a `Css`
    An extension on the `Css` companion, in `cataclysm.fonts`, taking a `Font in (? >:
    Web)` — a web font, or one whose provision is an embedded `Medium` one — and
    returning the stylesheet of `FontFace.rules` for it. Joined to a stylesheet's own
    rules with `+`, so a page carries the fonts its styles name; a generic family gives
    an empty stylesheet.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import formatting.compactCssFormatting

    given (Typeface of "Menlo") is Typesettable in Web = Web.local()
    val code: Font in Web = Web.font(Typeface["Menlo"].face)

    Css.fontFace(code) + css"pre { white-space: pre }"
    ```

    The declarations which select the face, `code.style`, are a `Css.Style` for an
    element's `style` attribute rather than a rule of the stylesheet.

entry .fontFaces   `Css.fontFaces(fonts*)`: the rules for several fonts, each typeface once, `@import`s first
    An extension on the `Css` companion, in `cataclysm.fonts`: `FontFace.stylesheet` over
    the fonts given, so a page which uses two faces of one typeface declares it once.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import formatting.compactCssFormatting

    given (Typeface of "Menlo") is Typesettable in Web = Web.local()
    val menlo = Typeface["Menlo"]

    Css.fontFaces(Web.font(menlo.face), Web.font(menlo.bold)).show
    // @font-face{font-family:"Menlo";src:local("Menlo");}
    ```

entry .style   the declarations which select a phoenicia `Face` or `Font`, as a `Css.Style`
    An extension on `Face`, and on `Font` (through its face), in `cataclysm.fonts`. The
    style always has `font-family` — a generic family unquoted, any other name quoted —
    and `font-weight`, and adds only what differs from the defaults: `font-style: italic`
    or `oblique 12deg` for a slant, `font-stretch` for a width, `font-variation-settings`
    for positions on the font's own axes, and `font-feature-settings` for the layout
    features enabled or disabled. The registered weight, width and italic axes go through
    the high-level properties, so a static and a variable font are asked for alike.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val inter = Typeface["Inter"]

    inter.bold.italic.style.text
    // font-family: "Inter"; font-weight: 700; font-style: italic
    Typeface.SansSerif.face.style.text
    // font-family: sans-serif; font-weight: 400
    inter.face.varying(Variation.Axis.OpticalSize, 14.0).disabling(Face.Feature.Ligatures).style.text
    // font-family: "Inter"; font-weight: 400; font-variation-settings: "opsz" 14.0; font-feature-settings: "liga" 0
    ```

    The result is a `Css.Style`, so it joins with another by `+` and serves as an
    honeycomb `style` attribute through `inlineStyle`.

entry .Styles   a stylesheet on the classpath, bound so that HTML class and id names are checked against it
  detail
      A `Styles` is a marker carrying a stylesheet's classpath path as its `Locus` type:
      `Styles(cp"/site.css")` is a `Styles at "/site.css"`. Brought into scope as a given,
      with `cssBindings.checkedBinding` imported, it makes every honeycomb attribution
      `Tag.name(…)` resolve `name` against the stylesheet's `classes` and `ids` as the code
      compiles: a class name attaches `class="name"`, an id `id="name"`, a name the
      stylesheet does not mention is a compile error, and a name which is both a class and
      an id is one too, so a misspelled class in a template cannot reach the browser.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      given (Styles at "/site.css") = Styles(cp"/site.css")
      import cssBindings.checkedBinding

      Div.button(t"Save")        // class="button", if .button is in the stylesheet
      Section.main(Div.button)   // id="main", if #main is
      ```

      The resource is read and parsed during macro expansion (once per path), so the
      stylesheet must be on the compiler's classpath, and the named given's type is
      parenthesised, as a given type with a literal must be. A stylesheet which cannot be
      read or parsed is a compile error naming the path. Only `cataclysm.html` has this;
      it depends on hellenism and honeycomb.
  refer cataclysm.cssBindings
  refer cataclysm.attributeFor
  refer cataclysm.classes
  entry .apply   `Styles(cp"/path.css")`: a marker whose `Locus` is the resource's path
      A macro which lifts the `cp"…"` resource's `Locus` into the result type, so
      `Styles(cp"/site.css")` is typed `Styles at "/site.css"`; a resource with no `Locus`
      is a compile error.

entry .attributeFor   the attribute, `class` or `id`, a name denotes in the `Styles` stylesheet in scope
    `attributeFor["button"]` expands to `t"class"` or `t"id"` according to which the
    in-scope `Styles` stylesheet declares, and fails to compile if the name is neither
    (_cataclysm: `name` is not a class or id in the stylesheet_), both (_… is both a class
    and an id in the stylesheet; rename one_), or no `Styles` given is in scope
    (_cataclysm: no stylesheet is in scope; add a `given Styles at "/path"`_). The name
    must be a string literal. `cssBindings.checkedBinding` is built on it.

entry .inlineStyle   lets a `Css.Style` be the value of a honeycomb `style` attribute
    The given `Css.Style is Attributive to Whatwg.Css`, in `cataclysm.html`, so
    `Div(style = Css.Style(color = red, width = 4.0*Px))` renders `style="color: #ff0000;
    width: 4px"`. It is a top-level given, named uniquely, and is in scope with
    `import soundness.*` only if imported by name (`import soundness.inlineStyle`) or
    through `import cataclysm.inlineStyle`.

entry .cssBindings   checked class and id names for HTML, a given to import by name
  detail
      The package holding `checkedBinding`, which turns honeycomb's `Tag.name(…)`
      attributions into names checked against the `Styles` stylesheet in scope. It is
      imported by name, after the `Styles` given is declared:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      given (Styles at "/site.css") = Styles(cp"/site.css")
      import cssBindings.checkedBinding
      ```
  entry .checkedBinding   makes `Tag.name(…)` attach `class="name"` or `id="name"` as the stylesheet in scope says
      An inline given `Attribution of name` for every literal name but `apply`, which
      expands through `attributeFor` to the right attribute, or to a compile error for a
      name the in-scope `Styles` stylesheet does not declare. Its unchecked counterpart is
      honeycomb's `stylesheets.uncheckedClasses`, which attaches `class="name"` for any
      name; the two are alternatives, each imported by name, and not both at once.
